  "title": "Chapter 8: METALS AND NON-METALS",
  "order": 28,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "oqfsfwu",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "PART 1 – THEORY"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Metals are electropositive elements that lose electrons to form cations, whereas non-metals are electronegative elements that gain or share electrons.",
        "Metals form basic or amphoteric oxides, while non-metals form acidic or neutral oxides.",
        "The reactivity series arranges metals in decreasing order of their chemical reactivity, dictating displacement reactions and extraction methods.",
        "Highly reactive metals (Na, K, Ca) are extracted via electrolysis, whereas moderately reactive metals (Fe, Zn) are extracted by reduction using carbon.",
        "Corrosion of iron (rusting) requires both oxygen and moisture, and can be prevented through galvanization, painting, or alloying."
      ],
      "id": "v3ax5rn"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
